----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHERE THIS SITS IN THE PUBLISHED LITERATURE (stated so the result is not mistaken for a discovery)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    The cluster residual surviving in LENSING is a known MOND result, not a new one.  Natarajan & Zhao 2008
    (MNRAS 389, 250) showed MOND plus classical neutrinos cannot supply cluster lensing; Angus, Famaey & Buote
    2008 (MNRAS 387, 1470) found unexplained mass on the group scale from X-ray profiles; and Famaey, Pizzuti &
    Saltas 2024 (arXiv:2410.02612) characterised the residual MOND missing mass directly from CLASH strong- and
    weak-lensing profiles, finding it 'in line with results obtained in the literature from the hydrostatic
    equilibrium of hot gas' -- an inner core with an outer slope steeper than -3.5 inside ~1 Mpc.
    What is new here is only that the test is run on THIS framework's own carried kernel (nu_RAR saturated),
    on THIS repository's own cluster sample and baryon budget, on both a0 footings, with the shortfall against
    lensing and against dynamics measured side by side at the same radii.  Note also that Famaey+2024's lensing
    shape is CORED, while this repository's own X-ray inversion (g04a) reports rho ~ r^-1.53 and NOT cored --
    a difference over different radial ranges and kernels, flagged here, not settled here.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
READING THE PATTERN
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    canonical: framework short of DYNAMICS by 1.62x, short of LENSING by 1.99x (4.63x with the shape correction, 2.68x in the raw observable), lensing/dynamics = 1.15 +/- 0.15
    alt: framework short of DYNAMICS by 1.49x, short of LENSING by 1.83x (4.14x with the shape correction, 2.48x in the raw observable), lensing/dynamics = 1.15 +/- 0.15
    Because the carried arm has gamma_PPN = 1, its lensing and dynamical predictions are the SAME number, so the
    two shortfalls can only differ by the amount the two MEASUREMENTS differ.  They differ by the hydrostatic bias
    and nothing else.  Three consequences, all structural:
      (a) the cluster residual is present at the same size in a probe with orthogonal systematics, so it is NOT an
          artefact of hydrostatic equilibrium -- L2/L5/L6/L7 do not rest on the HSE assumption;
      (b) NO choice of lensing sector can repair it.  A slip changes lensing and leaves dynamics alone; the
          shortfall is already present in dynamics.  The modified-inertia arm moves the wrong way besides:
          `prep_2026/mi_lensing_final/` derives g_lens <= g_bar for ANY baryonic source, i.e. M_lens <= M_bar here;
          for this sample that is a factor M_WL/M_bar = 6.6x, against the 1.8x the
          modified-gravity arm carries -- so cluster lensing excludes the MI arm independently of Brouwer 2021;
      (c) the SHAPE is a second, amplitude-free discriminator, and it is the framework's weakest point here: its
          phantom is far more extended than the measured convergence, so the observable an actual survey measures
          (DeltaSigma over 0.5-2 Mpc) is missed by more than the enclosed-mass comparison suggests.

  what this lane does NOT establish: it does not measure dark matter; it does not touch the galaxy-scale evidence,
  where baryons plus the kernel work; and with five clusters and 20-60% per-cluster weak-lensing errors it
  constrains a lensing-sector difference only at the ~15% level, so a slip smaller than that is untested, not
  excluded.  Seven of the twelve X-COP clusters have no published weak-lensing mass at all.

RESULT: 5 FAIL -> ["C6 [test] the framework's prediction matches the measured LENSING mass (shortfall consistent with 1)", "C7 [test] the framework's prediction matches the measured DYNAMICAL mass (the L2/L7 control, same radii)", 'C8 [THE TEST] the shortfall against LENSING differs from the shortfall against DYNAMICS at > 3 sigma (a lensing-sector signature)', "C11 [shape] the framework's predicted lensing profile SHAPE is consistent with the measured one over the weak-lensing fitting range", "C12 [as-obs] the NFW-refit shape systematic is under 15%, so comparing the framework's true M(<R500) with a published NFW-fit mass is a fair comparison"]
